   131	## 4. Frozen contracts
   132	
   133	These contracts are fixed before dependent implementation. Each is a design commitment; where its
   134	safety depends on unproven engine behavior, the linked falsifier must pass first, and a failed falsifier
   135	blocks the dependent work.
   136	
   137	### C1. Projection eligibility (both lanes)
   138	
   139	A read uses the projection only when every condition holds for the whole query; otherwise the whole
   140	query takes the exact ledger path in the same native language, and the fallback rate is reported.
   141	
   142	| Condition | XTDB | QLever |
   143	| --- | --- | --- |
   144	| Scope | the engine's ownership record matches the reader's scope (C7) | same |
   145	| Mapping | the projection's spec generation equals the release the reader requires | same |
   146	| Recorded time | R absent, or R at or after the recorded time of the newest published position | same |
   147	| Position | latest, or a physical commit boundary (H-X-CUT) | the current publication's P only |
   148	| Valid time | any V, read natively, for entities whose coordinates are exact | V inside the marker's `[validInstant, nextBoundary)` and the marker not in MAINTAINING |
   149	| Completeness | no partial, stale or conflicted entity touches an attribute the query references (filter, order, include, aggregate, negation) | same |
   150	
   151	Completeness is query-wide. If the per-type partial set intersects the query's referenced attributes,
   152	the query routes to the exact path before any filtering, ordering, aggregation or pagination. A disjoint
   153	union of projection and exact branches is a later optimization that needs its own proof.
   154	
   155	### C2. QLever durable commit protocol (H-Q-LOG)
   156	
   157	1. **Commit decision.** The broker appends a COMMIT record for a commit group to an append-only log and
   158	   fsyncs it. That fsync is the linearization point and the irrevocable decision: after it, recovery must
   159	   make the group visible; a committed command can never disappear.
   160	2. **Group validation before the decision.** Members are validated in order against a staged state equal
   161	   to the published state plus the earlier accepted members of the same group, including `EntityState`
   162	   and `TripleLive` preconditions, command claims, and unresolved durable claims from the log. A member
   163	   that fails is rejected alone. Nested savepoints and pending claims keep today's semantics.
   164	3. **Apply.** The broker applies the group as one SPARQL update operation carrying ledger insertions,
   165	   current-graph changes and the publication marker. The broker never cancels or times out its own
   166	   updates; it watches progress instead, because partial permutation states arise from cancellation.
   167	4. **Publication.** QLever's snapshot swap at the end of the operation is publication. Readers may see
   168	   the group from that moment. The caller's ACK is sent after publication.
   169	5. **Verification is post-publication.** The broker checks the complete expected effects and required
   170	   absences of the group. A failure means the published state is known-bad: the broker fences (C3).
   171	6. **Uncertainty.** Any non-success response, lost connection, process death or verification failure
   172	   fences the lane. Recovery restarts QLever, confirms the persisted delta matches a log prefix through
   173	   the complete expected effect sets, re-applies committed groups idempotently, or rebuilds from the
   174	   newest checkpoint plus log replay. Native reads return a typed "repairing" error while fenced.
   175	7. **Lost ACK.** A retry with the same command id finds the durable claim in the log and returns the
   176	   original receipt.
   177	8. **Replay and checkpoints.** The log is not an RDF snapshot. Replay is deterministic over a versioned
   178	   record format; checkpoints are graph-preserving N-Quads exports with a manifest (log prefix, format
   179	   versions, hashes); a fresh index is built from a checkpoint plus the log suffix; segment retention keeps
   180	   everything after the newest verified checkpoint. Rebuild publication uses crash-safe generation selection
   181	   (a new index directory, switched only after its manifest verifies), because the pinned source warns that
   182	   a failure during directory moves can need repair.
   183	9. **Profile name.** The qualified profile is "QLever 0.6.0 + log of record". Its native reads, including
   184	   every fallback, execute through declared SPARQL.
   185	
   186	Falsifiers that gate C2: F05 (fault injection between permutations, persistence, publication and
   187	verification), plus crash tests after every durable transition, competing preconditions in one group,
   188	duplicate commands within a group, failed children and lost ACKs.
   189	
   190	### C3. QLever mutation broker and fence
   191	
   192	1. **One mutator.** Every route that modifies QLever goes through the broker: application commits,
   193	   boundary maintenance, rebuild and checkpointing, administration. Native reads do not mutate (C8).
   194	2. **Credentials.** QLever runs with an update access token held only by the broker. No other process can
   195	   mutate the engine.
   196	3. **Epochs.** The broker holds a lease with an epoch number in the log. A new broker must close the
   197	   previous epoch (prove the old one is dead and its last group's outcome resolved) before it mutates.
   198	4. **Fence.** While fenced, no update and no rebuild starts until recovery has established a trustworthy
   199	   state from complete expected effects. Two successful queries are not that proof.
   200	
   201	### C4. Publication marker and temporal-boundary maintenance (QLever)
   202	
   203	1. The marker records `(P, validInstant, nextBoundary, state)`, with state `PUBLISHED` or `MAINTAINING`.
   204	2. Ordinary boundaries are commit groups that advance `validInstant` and `nextBoundary`.
   205	3. **Bounded operations.** Every update operation has a preconstruction bound on its size (the bound comes
   206	   from R8's group-commit curve). A boundary larger than the bound sets the marker to `MAINTAINING`, applies
   207	   the boundary in bounded chunks between application commit groups, and returns the marker to `PUBLISHED`
   208	   when done. While `MAINTAINING`, C1 sends repository reads to the exact path; raw queries see the
   209	   in-progress state and can read the marker.
   210	4. Delta growth is bounded by rebuild scheduling plus backpressure: when the forecast delta at rebuild
   211	   completion exceeds the cap, the broker slows admission of commit groups. A rebuild trigger alone is not
   212	   a bound.
   213	
   214	### C5. Conflict semantics (both lanes)
   215	
   216	1. **Equal duplicates are not conflicts.** Several live assertions with the same value form one value with
   217	   several supports, as the existing `EntityCodec` decodes them (`packages/http/src/EntityCodec.ts:203`).
   218	   Write-time cardinality enforcement keeps counting assertions (`Constraint.ts:193`); that rule is unchanged.
   219	2. **A conflict** is a cardinality-one attribute with two or more distinct live values at the basis. It can
   220	   exist with enforcement off, and also with enforcement on when it predates the enforcing write (enforcement
   221	   rejects only new or worsened violations).
   222	3. **Repository outcomes.** `get` and `getMany` fail with `ConflictingValuesError` listing every conflicted
   223	   entity and attribute. `find`, counts, ordering, includes and negation fail with the same error when a
   224	   referenced attribute is conflicted for any candidate entity; attributes the query does not reference do
   225	   not affect it. There is no three-valued "unknown".
   226	4. **Raw convenience shape.** XTDB scalar column NULL plus the attribute in `_triplex_conflicts`; QLever no
   227	   plain triple plus a conflict marker triple. This shape is documented as not equivalent for conflicted
   228	   attributes and never certifies ORM equivalence.
   229	5. **Canonical conflict-aware native relation.** The ledger-derived `values(attribute)` relation in each
   230	   lane's native context lists every live value with its assertion ids and supports.
   231	
   232	### C6. Journal contract
   233	
   234	The logical journal stays as documented: `_Transaction` facts, including `:_tx/change` and
   235	`:_tx/changed-entity`, remain queryable through Datalog and `match`, and `transactions()`,
   236	`transactionsForEntity` and `transactionByCommand` keep their outputs, order and `-0` behavior. Physical
   237	slimming is allowed only if the adapter synthesizes those facts on read with byte-identical results, tested by
   238	direct audit queries beyond the shared 44. A profile that drops them is a separately named breaking profile and
   239	cannot claim unchanged functionality. Until then the journal is stored as today and its cost is counted.
   240	
   241	### C7. Raw dataset and scope ownership
   242	
   243	1. **Production profile: one Triplex database scope per engine.** At initialization the engine stores an
   244	   ownership record (XTDB: `triplex_engine_owner`; QLever: a meta triple). The adapter refuses a mismatched
   245	   scope before touching unqualified projection storage.
   246	2. **Domain queries.** In QLever, domain predicates (`urn:triplex:attr:*`) and domain classes (`rdf:type
   247	   urn:triplex:type:*`) appear only in the current graph, so domain-pattern queries over the default union
   248	   graph match only current state. Unrestricted inspection such as `SELECT ?s ?p ?o` also sees ledger and meta
   249	   triples; that is documented. No staging graph ever holds domain predicates. In XTDB, domain tables carry plain
   250	   type names; ledger and metadata tables are `tx_*` and `triplex_*`.
   251	3. **Shared-engine profile (tests, multi-tenant dev).** Projection storage is scope-qualified, raw queries must
   252	   select their scope, and collision tests with identical entity ids in two scopes must show no leakage.
   253	
   254	### C8. Native reads never mutate
   255	
   256	Recursive and reachability native reads stop materializing scratch graphs or tables. They evaluate read-only:
   257	iterative frontier expansion in TypeScript, one native query per round with the frontier bound as parameters,
   258	under `maxRounds` and `maxRecursiveRows`. This removes a mutator class from both lanes. The affected native tasks
   259	are re-qualified under G2.
